% ============================================================================
% newton-laws — Newton's THREE laws of motion, keyed by law NUMBER (1, 2, 3),
% to the short name NASA gives each law (adj-facts-stdlib, PHYSICS).
% ============================================================================
% One of the first big ideas of mechanics, and a rung a physics agent reasons
% UP from: classical motion rests on exactly THREE laws, and each one has a
% number and a short name. "The first law is the law of inertia; the second law
% is the law of force (F = m·a); the third law is the law of action and
% reaction." Before you can reason about a rocket, a collision, or a free-body
% diagram you must first know WHICH of the three numbered laws you are invoking.
% Recall is a binding query keyed by the law's NUMBER:
%
%     ? newton_law(2, $Name)      % force
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `newton_law(number, name)` carrying the table's citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the short
% name WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on any
% law number NASA does not fix (there is no fourth law of motion).
%
% Each of the three laws and the short name NASA prints for it, as a little
% truth table (the KEY is the bare law NUMBER, exactly like temperature-scales
% keys on a bare number):
%
%     law number   short name         what NASA labels it
%     ----------   ----------------   -------------------------------------
%     1            inertia            "Newton's First Law of Motion (Inertia)"
%     2            force              "Newton's Second Law of Motion (Force)"
%     3            action_reaction    "Newton's Third Law of Motion (Action & Reaction)"
%
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a name and recall its law number:
%
%     ? newton_law($N, action_reaction)   % 3 — reverse recall
%
% A number that is NOT one of these three — `4`, `0` — is deliberately NOT a
% row, so a law recall ABSTAINS on it rather than inventing a fourth law. That
% honest-abstention property is what makes the table safe to build a reasoner
% on: it answers exactly the number→name pairs it can ground, and only those.
%
% The `name` value of every row is a SINGLE lowercase snake_case atom, copied
% from the parenthetical short label NASA prints beside each numbered law —
% never a name the source does not support. (NASA writes the third law's label
% "Action & Reaction"; the atom normalizes the ampersand to `action_reaction`,
% so every value stays literally checkable against the quoted span.)
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (nothing here is asserted from memory — every number→name pair
% below was read out of a fetched NASA Glenn Research Center page this session,
% and any pair whose name could not be verified there would have been dropped).
% All three names come from the SAME primary U.S. government source, NASA's
% Beginner's Guide to Aeronautics "Newton's Laws of Motion"
% (www1.grc.nasa.gov/beginners-guide-to-aeronautics/newtons-laws-of-motion/),
% whose numbered review list labels each law, quoted here character-for-
% character so any reader can re-check it:
%
%   1 → inertia
%     "Newton's First Law of Motion (Inertia)"
%
%   2 → force
%     "Newton's Second Law of Motion (Force)"
%     (the same page states the second law as "The acceleration of an object
%      depends on the mass of the object and the amount of force applied." and
%      gives the formula "F = m · a")
%
%   3 → action_reaction
%     "Newton's Third Law of Motion (Action & Reaction)"
%     (the same page states the third law as "Whenever one object exerts a force
%      on another object, the second object exerts an equal and opposite force
%      on the first.")
%
% An ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance envelope, so the `source`/`locator`/
% `trust` below hold the single cleanest span: the NASA label that fixes the
% first row (1 → inertia). Every OTHER row's per-fact source and verbatim span
% is listed above, so each name remains independently checkable. The source is a
% primary U.S. government NASA (Glenn Research Center) page, so `trust
% authoritative`.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts, feedback_no_byte_arithmetic_for_llm.)
% ============================================================================

table newton_law {
    columns number, name

    row (1, inertia)
    row (2, force)
    row (3, action_reaction)

    source "Newton's First Law of Motion (Inertia)"
    locator "https://www1.grc.nasa.gov/beginners-guide-to-aeronautics/newtons-laws-of-motion/"
    trust authoritative
}
